(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-2-cam-nutrition-exam2-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CAM/Nutrition Exam 2 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"CAM / Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which indications does the lecture give for equine-assisted psychotherapy?","choices":["Cerebral palsy, neuromuscular disorders, and physical rehabilitation goals","Anxiety, post-traumatic stress disorder, antisocial disorders, and grief","Corporate leadership, team-building, and organisational communication goals","Visual impairment, hearing impairment, and other sensory disabilities"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these indications and notes studies suggest it may improve trust and secure attachment in traumatised youth. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe acupuncture licensure across the United States?","choices":["It requires a medical or osteopathic degree in every state","It differs by state, with 47 of 50 states licensing acupuncturists","It is unregulated in every state","It is federally licensed through a single national board"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture also gives more than 38,000 licensed acupuncturists in the United States. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which organisations does the lecture name as equine-assisted therapy certifying bodies?","choices":["The American Hippotherapy Association and the Americans with Disabilities Act","The National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health","The Fair Housing Authority and the Department of Transportation","The Professional Association of Therapeutic Horsemanship International and Eagala"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names PATH International and Eagala as equine-assisted therapy certifying organisations. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which guideline does the lecture give for using essential oils?","choices":["Store them in sunlight so the oils remain active","Essential oils are powerful, so less is better","Apply them undiluted for the strongest effect","Any quality of oil produces the same effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture also directs you to keep bottles tightly closed, dilute with lotion for skin use, and know the oil's properties and quality. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"The lecture credits Hippocrates with which of the following?","choices":["Founding the first naturopathic medical college in North America","Being called the Father of Medicine and saying \"Let food be thy medicine\"","Developing the law of similars that underlies homeopathic prescribing","Establishing the meridian system through which the vital force flows"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture calls Hippocrates one of the most outstanding figures in the history of medicine, often referred to as the Father of Medicine, and credits him with the saying \"Let food be thy medicine.\" — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which adjustment technique does the lecture identify as the most common?","choices":["Gonstead, a side lying manual technique","Diversified, using manual pressure with multiple modalities","Thompson, a low force technique using a drop table","Activator Method, using a handheld low force instrument"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists it first and marks it as most common. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the lecture say determines how dark the marks left by cupping are?","choices":["The temperature of the flame used to create the vacuum","The greater the build-up of toxins, metabolic waste, or blood stagnation","The patient's baseline skin tone and complexion alone","The number of cups applied to the area at once"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture adds that how long the discolouration lasts depends on how quickly the body can cleanse itself. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Which herb does the lecture pair with a specific warning about use on the face of infants and young children?","choices":["Arnica","Lavender","Capsaicin","Peppermint"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture warns that peppermint oil should not be applied to the face of infants or young children because inhaling the menthol may cause serious side effects. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about referred pain from trigger points?","choices":["It follows dermatomal boundaries exactly","It follows consistent patterns","It is unpredictable and varies between patients","It is always felt at the site of the point itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs consistent referred pain patterns with consistent point locations that are easily treated with acupuncture. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which physiologic functions does the lecture list as monitorable during biofeedback?","choices":["Serum electrolyte concentration, blood glucose, and circulating hormone levels","Heart rate, blood pressure, muscle activity, skin temperature, breathing, and brain activity","Bone mineral density, joint range of motion, and overall muscular strength","Visual acuity, auditory threshold, and peripheral sensation to light touch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these physiologic functions as measurable by the electronic monitoring devices used in biofeedback. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A patient stops his prescribed antihypertensive entirely and treats his blood pressure only with herbal preparations. This is best classified as which of the following?","choices":["Complementary medicine, since herbal products supplement the conventional regimen","Alternative medicine, since the therapy is used in place of conventional treatment","Integrative medicine, since more than one treatment modality is now involved","Functional medicine, since an underlying contributor is being addressed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines alternative medicine as an approach used in place of conventional medicine, which is precisely what replacing the antihypertensive represents. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which benefit does the lecture attribute to Tai Chi?","choices":["Reduced fall risk in older adults","Reversal of established osteoarthritis","Cure of chronic respiratory disease","Replacement of prescribed medication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture also lists decreased stress, anxiety and blood pressure, and increased aerobic capacity, energy, flexibility, balance, and strength. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"How does the lecture define aromatherapy?","choices":["The use of essential oils for therapeutic purposes","The use of scented candles and diffusers to promote relaxation","The inhalation of steam from diluted herbal teas","The application of dry heat over acupuncture points"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It uses concentrated herbal organic compounds from plants, in association with massage, inhalation, oral consumption, compresses and baths. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which physiological processes does the lecture say the endocannabinoid system modulates?","choices":["Only pain perception and appetite","Pain, seizure threshold, appetite, digestion and mood","Only immune function and inflammatory response","Thermoregulation and blood pressure control exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists modulation of pain, seizure threshold, appetite, digestion, mood and others, and says it may play a role in intraocular pressure. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What does chiropractic care centre on, according to the lecture?","choices":["The diagnosis and surgical correction of spinal deformity","The prescription of medication for musculoskeletal pain","The rehabilitation of patients after orthopaedic surgery","Diagnosis, treatment and prevention of neuro-musculoskeletal disorders"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture opens with exactly this definition. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What does the lecture say horses seek in a handler?","choices":["A leader they can trust and who will protect them","A handler who matches their level of physical strength","A consistent feeding schedule above all else","An environment free of other horses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states horses establish boundaries and seek a leader they can trust and will protect them. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"During which techniques may a medium interfere with the intended result?","choices":["Petrissage and friction massage","No technique is affected by the medium used","Effleurage and tapotement","Only during effleurage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mediums may interfere with kneading and lifting in petrissage, and with the results sought in friction massage. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What is moxibustion, as the lecture describes it?","choices":["Burning dried mugwort on or near the skin or needle to apply heat","Applying heated glass cups to the skin to create a vacuum","Scraping the skin firmly with a smooth-edged tool","Passing a low electrical current between two inserted needles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture dates its use to 500 BCE and gives pain, tendonitis, digestive issues, and menstrual problems as indications. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which interaction does the lecture attribute to garlic that is relevant to HIV treatment?","choices":["It raises levels of antiretroviral drugs to toxic concentrations","It interferes with the effectiveness of indinavir","It interferes with the effectiveness of saquinavir","It has no documented interaction with antiretroviral agents"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states garlic has been found to interfere with the effectiveness of saquinavir, a drug used to treat HIV infection, and that garlic can thin the blood similarly to aspirin. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which environmental exposure source does the lecture pair correctly with its metal?","choices":["Well water and rice are common sources of environmental lead exposure","Old paint and plumbing are common sources of environmental arsenic exposure","Fish is a common source of environmental mercury exposure","Leafy vegetables are a common source of environmental cadmium exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists lead in old paint and plumbing, arsenic in well water and rice, and mercury in fish as common sources of environmental metal exposure. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which category accounts for the largest share of the world literature on acupuncture treatment, according to the lecture?","choices":["Psychiatric conditions, at 5 percent","Pain problems, at 25 percent","Organic lesions, at 40 percent","Surgical anaesthesia, at 10 percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The remainder is surgical anaesthesia and neurological at 10 percent each, then substance abuse, psychiatric, and miscellaneous at 5 percent each. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which complication does the lecture name when warning about risky points?","choices":["Cauda equina syndrome","Cerebrovascular accident","Compartment syndrome","Pneumothorax"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives pneumothorax as the example of a risk requiring care with point selection. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What does a guide dog or Seeing Eye dog do?","choices":["Alerts to sounds a deaf handler cannot hear","Detects the onset of panic attacks and anxiety episodes","Navigation and environmental awareness for people who are blind","Provides emotional comfort and companionship without task training"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines the guide dog or Seeing Eye dog in exactly these terms. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What training does the law require of an emotional support animal?","choices":["Certification by the Professional Association of Therapeutic Horsemanship International","A minimum of 100 hours of supervised task training","Completion of a professional service animal training program","Training is not required by law"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states training is not required by law for an emotional support animal, though a licensed healthcare or mental health professional may recommend one and provide supporting documentation. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"With which drug classes does the lecture say interactions should be expected?","choices":["Only the anticoagulants and antiplatelet agents","Only those drugs metabolised by cytochrome P-450 2D6","Sympathomimetics, central nervous system depressants, anticholinergics","No interactions are expected because cannabis is not hepatically metabolised"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names these classes and gives alcohol, benzodiazepines, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors and zolpidem as examples, noting few data exist and predictions rest on cytochrome P-450. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which pulmonary and vascular concerns does the lecture raise about cannabis?","choices":["It can worsen bronchitis and pneumonia, with possible vascular risk","It improves pulmonary symptoms through bronchodilation","It has no cardiovascular associations at any dose","It causes pulmonary fibrosis in most long-term users"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists worsening pulmonary symptoms including bronchitis and pneumonia, and a possible association with myocardial infarction, stroke and peripheral vascular disease, adding that cannabis can exacerbate arrhythmia. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A patient continues her oncologist's chemotherapy regimen and also attends weekly acupuncture sessions for nausea. Using the lecture's terminology, the acupuncture is best described as which of the following?","choices":["Complementary medicine, because it is used together with conventional medicine","Alternative medicine, because it is used in place of conventional medicine","Conventional medicine, because a licensed clinician recommended it","Allopathic medicine, because it is delivered inside a hospital system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines complementary medicine as an approach used TOGETHER with conventional medicine, which is exactly what adding acupuncture to an ongoing chemotherapy regimen represents. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which form of licorice does the lecture name for canker sores, peptic ulcers and reflux?","choices":["Whole licorice root taken as a tea","Licorice combined with bromelain","Deglycyrrhizinated licorice","Licorice extract standardised to glycyrrhizin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names deglycyrrhizinated licorice for canker sores, peptic ulcers and gastro-oesophageal reflux disease, alongside traditional use for cough, asthma and sore throat. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which element does the lecture pair with the stomach and spleen?","choices":["Wood","Earth","Metal","Fire"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Wood covers gallbladder and liver, fire covers small intestine, heart, pericardium and Sanjiao, metal covers lung and large intestine, and water covers bladder and kidney. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What began at Pawling Convalescent Hospital in New York in 1944?","choices":["The first certification examination for hippotherapy practitioners","The first documented use of horses for psychotherapy","A rehabilitation programme for veterans working with farm animals and dogs","A federal registry of emotional support animals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes exactly this programme at Pawling Convalescent Hospital in 1944. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What did the December 2020 Department of Transportation rule change about air travel?","choices":["It required all airlines to accommodate emotional support animals free of charge","It limited service animals to trained dogs, excluding support animals","It extended service animal status to miniature horses on aircraft","It removed all training requirements for service animals in flight"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture summarises the final rule revising the Air Carrier Access Act regulation in exactly these terms. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A clinician teaches a patient to systematically tense and then release muscle groups throughout the body. Which technique is this?","choices":["Progressive muscle relaxation, which helps patients recognize and reduce muscle tension","Guided imagery, which uses mental images and several senses to build a calming scene","Electromyographic biofeedback, which converts muscle signals into a visible display","Transcendental meditation, which uses silent repetition of a mantra during practice"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists progressive muscle relaxation as a relaxation technique in which patients systematically tense and relax muscle groups, helping them recognize and reduce tension. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What effect do light, slow massage techniques produce?","choices":["Increased blood circulation to the area","An increase in cardiac output","A rise in blood pressure","Systemic relaxation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fast, deep techniques instead increase blood circulation to the area. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What proportion of United States adults have used acupuncture, according to the World Health Organization figure in the lecture?","choices":["More than 6.5 percent","More than 16 percent","More than 25 percent","More than 40 percent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture also gives more than 10 million treatments annually and use in more than 103 countries. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What common goal do all the adjustment techniques share, according to the lecture?","choices":["To restore normal motion and normalise the nervous system","To achieve an audible release at every treated segment","To avoid the need for any imaging","To reduce the number of visits a patient needs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture makes this the shared goal despite the variety of techniques. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Who may write an emotional support animal letter, per the lecture?","choices":["A physician or a licensed mental health professional","Any landlord or a property manager acting for them","A certified professional animal trainer","The Florida Department of Health directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the letter is written by a licensed professional, a physician or licensed mental health professional, documenting the need for the animal as part of mental health treatment. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which condition does the lecture list among the indications for hypnotherapy?","choices":["Irritable bowel syndrome","Iron overload requiring metal binding","Lead poisoning from environmental exposure","Pelvic floor dysfunction managed by muscle sensors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists irritable bowel syndrome among hypnotherapy indications, alongside acute and chronic pain, anxiety, phobias, nausea and vomiting, procedure-related distress, habit disorders, insomnia, and childbirth support. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the primary lesion addressed in chiropractic, according to the lecture?","choices":["Nerve root compression caused by a herniated disc","Muscle imbalance acting across a single joint","Inflammation of the facet joint capsule and its lining","Intersegmental dysfunction within the spinal column itself"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines this as discrete areas of hypomobility within the articulations of the spine. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which unusual animal interventions does the lecture show as examples?","choices":["Dolphin swimming and falconry","Goat yoga and snake massage","Llama trekking and beekeeping","Aquarium therapy and birdwatching"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture includes goat yoga and snake massage as examples. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slides 6 and 7"},{"q":"Where are CB1 receptors predominantly found, and what does that explain?","choices":["Mainly in the immune system, explaining the anti-inflammatory effects","Mainly in hepatic tissue, explaining first-pass metabolism","Mainly in the gastrointestinal tract, explaining the antiemetic effects","Mainly in the central nervous system, explaining THC psychoactivity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture places CB1 receptors in the central nervous system and throughout the brain, which accounts for THC's psychoactive effects, and also notes immune, gastrointestinal, reproductive, adrenal, heart, lung and bladder tissue. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Where does the lecture say homeopathy is least helpful?","choices":["In chronic disease such as asthma and eczema","In anatomical abnormalities needing surgery, or with obstacles to cure","In behavioural problems","In recurring infections in children"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Obstacles to cure include excessive drugs, an abusive lifestyle, and end-stage disease. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which question does the lecture recommend clinicians raise when a patient is considering a complementary therapy?","choices":["Does the patient's family approve of the proposed therapy?","Does the therapy require patients to stop conventional medical treatments?","Was the therapy first described in writing before the beginning of the twentieth century?","Is the therapy available in more than one country worldwide?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is one of the lecture's explicit counseling questions, along with asking about research support, substantial side effects, unfounded claims, travel, licensure, institutional backing, and expense. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what is Qi and how is it believed to travel?","choices":["A dosha determined at birth that travels through the bloodstream","A vital force of life that flows along pathways called meridians","A biofield generated by the practitioner and transferred through the hands","An electrical signal measured by sensors placed on the skin surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes Qi, pronounced \"chee,\" as a vital force of life that travels along pathways called meridians, sometimes described as an energy highway. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which neurotransmitters does the lecture list as released by acupuncture?","choices":["Acetylcholine, histamine, bradykinin, prostaglandins, and substance P","Insulin, cortisol, growth hormone, thyroxine, and aldosterone","Substance P, calcitonin gene-related peptide, and vasoactive intestinal peptide","Endorphins, serotonin, enkephalins, gamma-aminobutyric acid, norepinephrine, and dopamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture groups these under central effects alongside local, segmental, and descending inhibition. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which condition appears on the Florida qualifying list given in the lecture?","choices":["Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis is among the qualifying medical conditions listed per the Florida Department of Health Office of Medical Marijuana Use. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What does the lecture require for a terminal condition to qualify a patient in Florida?","choices":["Certification by the same physician who issues the recommendation","A hospice referral made within the previous 30 days","Diagnosis by a physician other than the certifying one","Written confirmation by the Florida Department of Health"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The qualifying list includes a terminal condition diagnosed by a physician other than the qualified physician issuing the physician certification, documented by a second physician. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slides 56 and 69"},{"q":"What does the lecture say happens through potentisation?","choices":["The remedy gradually loses all therapeutic activity","Only the taste and smell of the remedy are altered","Inert substances become active and toxic ones non-toxic","The remedy becomes progressively more concentrated at each step"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the remedy as being in a vibrational or quantum form, no longer governed by Newtonian physics. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"What proportion of supplements sold online does the FDA estimate to be counterfeit?","choices":["About one in every ten adults","About two out of every three adults","About one in every three adults","About one in every twenty adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture cites an FDA estimate that two out of every three supplements sold online is a counterfeit. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which receptors make up the endocannabinoid system as described in the lecture?","choices":["Two G-protein coupled receptors, CB1 and CB2, plus enzymes","Two ligand-gated ion channels, CB1 and CB2, plus transport proteins","A single G-protein coupled receptor with two binding sites","Two nuclear hormone receptors that alter gene transcription"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the endocannabinoid system as two G-protein coupled receptors, CB1 and CB2, plus other regulatory enzymes. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which of the following does the lecture list as a miscellaneous pathogenic factor rather than an exogenous one?","choices":["Damp","Summer heat","Stress","Cold"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The miscellaneous factors are irregular food intake, stress, lack of exercise, traumatic injuries, and stagnant blood or phlegm. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which statement reflects the lecture's definition of hypnosis?","choices":["A sleep state during which the patient loses awareness of the surroundings","A state of focused attention and increased responsiveness to therapeutic suggestion","A condition in which the therapist assumes control of the patient's decisions","A form of entertainment without any recognized clinical application"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines hypnosis as a state of focused attention with reduced peripheral awareness and an enhanced capacity for response to suggestion. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What are the five branches of Traditional Chinese Medicine as the lecture lists them?","choices":["Acupuncture, herbalism, massage or tuina, diet or nutrition, and movement","Acupuncture, surgery, herbalism, meditation, and prayer","Acupuncture, cupping, moxibustion, gua sha, and massage","Herbalism, homeopathy, massage, diet, and movement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The movement branch covers Tai Chi Chuan and Qigong. — Acupuncture 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"On what belief are energy therapies based, and what does the lecture say about that basis?","choices":["That the measurable electrical currents generated within skeletal muscle can be deliberately retrained, a mechanism that is now well established experimentally","That plant compounds act on receptor systems, which has been confirmed pharmacologically","That biofields influence health and healing, though their existence and clinical significance have not been conclusively demonstrated scientifically","That structural misalignment causes disease, which imaging studies have verified"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states energy therapies rest on the belief that biofields influence health and healing, and adds that the existence and clinical significance of subtle biofields have not been conclusively demonstrated scientifically. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What does the lecture say happened to massage in Europe during the Middle Ages?","choices":["It was formally licensed for the first time","It was discouraged as a healing practice by the Church of Rome","It was restricted to use on athletes only","It became the dominant treatment in monastic infirmaries"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture contrasts this with its earlier use in Greece and Rome. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What does the lecture say modern research suggests about how acupuncture may work?","choices":["It corrects measurable deficiencies of trace minerals in the bloodstream","It produces its effects entirely through patient expectation with no physiologic correlate","It works by diluting and shaking the active substance before administration","It may influence pain-processing pathways and benefit selected pain conditions and symptom management"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes that in traditional Chinese medicine acupuncture is believed to influence the flow of Qi along meridians, while modern research suggests it may influence pain-processing pathways. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What did Florence Nightingale recommend in 1860, according to the lecture?","choices":["Structured horseback riding for physical rehabilitation","Farm animal work for wounded and disabled veterans","Certification requirements for therapy animal handlers","Small companion animals such as birds, as an adjunct to healing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture credits Nightingale in 1860 with recommending small companion animals, birds specifically, as an adjunct to healing, especially for the chronically ill. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which three equine-assisted modalities does the lecture name?","choices":["Hippotherapy, equine-assisted psychotherapy, and equine-facilitated learning","Hippotherapy, therapeutic riding, and vaulting","Equine-assisted psychotherapy, goat yoga, and snake massage","Hippotherapy, service animal training, and emotional support animal placement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these three equine-assisted therapies. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is the stated purpose of the adjustment?","choices":["To restore normal movement and resolve segmental hypomobility","To stretch the spinal cord and its coverings","To permanently fuse the affected segment","To reduce inflammation by increasing local blood flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Those effects are biomechanical, physiological and neurological. — ChiropracticHomeopathicMassageAromatherapy 2026 Mattox.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which cues might a psychiatric service dog respond to, per the lecture?","choices":["Pacing, tremors, breathing changes, repetitive behaviours","Blood glucose changes detected by scent on the breath","Rising ambient noise levels in crowded public spaces","Loss of balance while walking over uneven ground"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these as cues, with responses including nudging the handler, retrieving medication, or guiding the handler to a safe location. — STUDENT Animal-Assisted Therapy Dr. McInnis 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why does stopping cannabis take a few weeks to relieve cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome?","choices":["Because cannabinoid receptors must be regenerated","Because antiemetics must first be withdrawn","Because hepatic enzyme induction persists for a month","Because of fat deposits"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that stopping cannabis consumption takes a few weeks due to fat deposits. — Herbal Medicines and Medical Marijuana- HAUSER 2026.pptx, Slide 73"}]};
